## 10. PAYMENT METHODS: COD, EASYPAISA AND JAZZCASH

Create a dedicated Payment Settings page in the admin dashboard.

Each payment method must be independently configurable.

### Cash on Delivery

Admin controls:
- Enable or disable COD.
- Set a COD fee.
- Set minimum and maximum order values.
- Restrict COD by delivery area if needed.
- Set customer instructions.
- Configure whether order confirmation is required before fulfillment.

At checkout:
- Allow customers to choose COD when eligible.
- Record the order as payment pending.
- Clearly display the amount due on delivery.
- Store the selected payment method.

Allow authorized admins to mark COD payments as collected, failed, or refunded where appropriate.

Do not mark COD as paid when the order is merely created or shipped.

### Easypaisa

Provide:
- Enable and disable controls.
- Configurable display name and instructions.
- Merchant account configuration for an approved integration.
- Secure gateway configuration where supported.
- Manual payment reference submission when an approved automated integration is unavailable.
- Admin verification workflow.
- Payment status history.

### JazzCash

Provide the same configuration capabilities:
- Enable and disable controls.
- Configurable instructions.
- Approved merchant gateway integration where available.
- Secure credential management.
- Manual transaction reference workflow when necessary.
- Admin verification and payment status history.

### Payment configuration rules

- Allow the admin to enable or disable COD, Easypaisa, and JazzCash independently.
- Update checkout options immediately after settings are saved.
- Do not require redeployment for ordinary payment-setting changes.
- Keep secret API credentials in server-side environment variables or a secure secrets manager.
- Do not expose credentials in frontend JavaScript, public database records, or browser responses.
- Never request customer PINs, OTPs, passwords, or payment card security codes.
- Treat manual payment references as unverified until an authorized admin verifies them.
- Use provider webhooks and signature verification when supported.
- Make payment verification idempotent so the same event cannot credit an order twice.
- Record failed payment attempts and verification actions.
- Clearly label any payment method that still requires merchant approval or configuration.

Create a test mode for development. Never display a fake successful payment for a real transaction.

